## Why This Is Asked

This question assesses practical understanding of timer-driven thread wakeups, race condition handling, and efficient data structures in a kernel-like environment. It tests a candidate's ability to design a concrete mechanism that works correctly under contention while maintaining precise timing guarantees.

## Key Concepts

- Sleep queues and timer interrupt handling
- Min-heap data structure for efficient wake-time ordering
- Concurrency safety and time rechecking to mitigate timer jitter
- Correctness guarantees: ensuring no missed wakeups and exact timing

## Code Example

```c
// Minimal sketch of a per-CPU sleep queue
typedef struct SleepEntry {
  uint64_t wake_at;
  int tid;
  struct SleepEntry *next;
} SleepEntry;

// Enqueue thread for timed sleep
void sleep(int tid, uint64_t duration) {
  uint64_t now = get_time();
  SleepEntry entry = {now + duration, tid};
  minheap_insert(&cpu->sleep_queue, entry);
  block_thread(tid);
}

// Timer interrupt handler
void timer_interrupt() {
  uint64_t now = get_time();
  while (!minheap_empty(&cpu->sleep_queue)) {
    SleepEntry entry = minheap_peek(&cpu->sleep_queue);
    if (entry.wake_at > now) break;

    entry = minheap_pop(&cpu->sleep_queue);
    if (entry.wake_at <= now) {
      wake_thread(entry.tid);
    } else {
      minheap_insert(&cpu->sleep_queue, entry);
    }
  }
}
```

## Overview
This question evaluates understanding of inter-thread synchronization, producer-consumer patterns, and low-level data structure design in operating system environments.

## Key Concepts
- Circular buffer with head/tail indices and wraparound handling
- Mutexes and condition variables for blocking operations and wakeups
- Memory visibility guarantees via compiler and hardware memory barriers
- Correctness and performance characteristics with multiple concurrent producers

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct {
    char data[LOG_SIZE];
    int valid;
} slot_t;

slot_t buffer[N];
int head = 0;
int tail = 0;
int count = 0;

mutex_t mtx;
cond_t not_full;
cond_t not_empty;

void enqueue(const char *record) {
    mutex_lock(&mtx);
    while (count >= N)
        cond_wait(&not_full, &mtx);
    buffer[tail % N] = *(slot_t *)record;
    tail++;
    count++;
    cond_signal(&not_empty);
    mutex_unlock(&mtx);
}

void dequeue(char *record) {
    mutex_lock(&mtx);
    while (count == 0)
        cond_wait(&not_empty, &mtx);
    *(slot_t *)record = buffer[head % N];
    head++;
    count--;
    cond_signal(&not_full);
    mutex_unlock(&mtx);
}
```

## Test Plan
1. **Correctness**: Verify no data loss or corruption with single producer/consumer
2. **Blocking**: Confirm enqueue blocks when buffer is full, dequeue blocks when empty
3. **Throughput**: Measure records/second with 1, 4, 8, and 16 concurrent producers
4. **Stress**: Run sustained test to detect race conditions and memory leaks

